The Right to Trial by Jury
The Seventh Amendment guarantees a constitutional right to trial by jury in civil cases. Note that the Seventh Amendment does not provide an unqualified or universal right to trial by jury in civil cases. Read the Seventh Amendment carefully. What scope does it define for this right?

U.S. Constitution, Amendment VII
Ratified December 15, 1791

In Suits at common law, where the value in controversy shall exceed twenty dollars, the right of trial by jury shall be preserved * * * .



•In general, cases that were brought in law courts in 1791 have a right to trial by jury

•In general, cases that were brought in equity courts in 1791 do not have a right to trial by jury

NOTES AND QUESTIONS ON THE SEVENTH AMENDMENT
1. The Seventh Amendment guarantees a right to jury trial in some—but not all—civil cases. Note that it does not say, “In all civil cases.” It says, “In Suits at common law.” Recall from our review of the history of pleading that prior to the Federal Rules, federal courts (and before them, English and colonial courts) observed a distinction between cases at common law and cases in equity. So law suits are within the scope of the Seventh Amendment, while equity suits are not. This yields the question: How does this distinction operate in the modern era, where the Federal Rules have merged all suits in law or equity into a single action (i.e., the civil action)?

2. Note, too, that the Seventh Amendment does not say, “there shall be a right to trial by jury.” It says, “the right of trial by jury shall be preserved.” This indicates that the amendment was not creating a new right or expanding
475
an existing right but preserving a right that existed at that time—which was 1791. This raises the question: How does the limitation of the amendment to preserving an existing right affect how the Seventh Amendment applies to legal claims that didn’t exist in 1791, but that plaintiffs can bring today?

3. The Seventh Amendment includes a lower limit on the stakes of a case with a right to jury trial. The “value in controversy” must “exceed twenty dollars.” Twenty dollars meant something different in 1791 than it does today. The average wage for a farm or city worker in the United States in 1791 was about 50 cents per day or $2.50 per week. This made twenty dollars about eight times the typical weekly wage. Today, median wages in the U.S. are about $1,000 per week.* Eight times the typical weekly wage today would be $8,000. Courts have not, however, attempted to update the amount-in-controversy requirement in the Seventh Amendment to account for economic growth or inflation. Why might it make sense to account for the changing value of a dollar in interpreting the Seventh Amendment? Why might it make sense not to do so?

INTRODUCTORY NOTE TO RULE 38 AND RULE 39
Rules 38 and 39 provide the parameters under which parties may invoke a right to trial by jury as a matter of federal procedure. As you read these Rules, ask yourself: When can a party obtain a jury trial, even when the Seventh Amendment does not create a right to jury trial? When can a party, even in a case for which the Seventh Amendment creates a right to jury trial, forfeit that right?

Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 38
Right to a Jury Trial; Demand

(a) Right Preserved. The right of trial by jury as declared by the Seventh Amendment to the Constitution—or as provided by a federal statute—is preserved to the parties inviolate.

476
(b) Demand. On any issue triable of right by a jury, a party may demand a jury trial by:

(1) serving the other parties with a written demand—which may be included in a pleading—no later than 14 days after the last pleading directed to the issue is served * * * .

(c) Specifying Issues. In its demand, a party may specify the issues that it wishes to have tried by a jury; otherwise, it is considered to have demanded a jury trial on all the issues so triable. If the party has demanded a jury trial on only some issues, any other party may—within 14 days after being served with the demand or within a shorter time ordered by the court—serve a demand for a jury trial on any other or all factual issues triable by jury.

(d) Waiver; Withdrawal. A party waives a jury trial unless its demand is properly served and filed. A proper demand may be withdrawn only if the parties consent.

* * *

Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 39
Trial by Jury or by the Court

(a) When a Demand Is Made. When a jury trial has been demanded under Rule 38, the action must be designated on the docket as a jury action. The trial on all issues so demanded must be by jury unless:

(1) the parties or their attorneys file a stipulation to a nonjury trial or so stipulate on the record; or

(2) the court, on motion or on its own, finds that on some or all of those issues there is no federal right to a jury trial.

(b) When No Demand Is Made. Issues on which a jury trial is not properly demanded are to be tried by the court. But the court may, on motion, order a jury trial on any issue for which a jury might have been demanded.

(c) Advisory Jury; Jury Trial by Consent. In an action not triable of right by a jury, the court, on motion or on its own:

477
(1) may try any issue with an advisory jury; or

(2) may, with the parties’ consent, try any issue by a jury whose verdict has the same effect as if a jury trial had been a matter of right, unless the action is against the United States and a federal statute provides for a nonjury trial.



•Congress can give right to jury trial by statute, and Rules provide for right to jury trial when granted by statute or the Seventh Amendment

•If the parties consent, the court may (but is not required to) order a binding trial by jury even on issues where there is no federal right to a jury trial

NOTES AND QUESTIONS ON RULE 38 AND RULE 39
1. Most of this chapter’s discussion of the right to trial by jury revolves around the constitutional right to trial by jury in a civil action, as provided by the Seventh Amendment. But Rules 38 and 39 inform us that there are other means by which a party can obtain a jury trial in a civil case. One means is that a statute may create a right to trial by jury. For example, Congress, when it creates a cause of action by statute, may specify that parties shall have a right to trial by jury for claims under the statute. As a normative matter, if you were advising Congress, for what types of federal rights would you want to see (or not want to see) a right to jury trial?

Another way a party can get a jury trial is by obtaining agreement from the other party (or parties) and the judge. Put yourself in the shoes of a district judge in a case where there is no statutory or constitutional right to a jury trial, but both parties have requested a trial by jury. What considerations might lead you to grant the request, or to deny it?

